    {
      "type": "text",
      "title": "📖 How to Observe Multi-UE Registration Impact",
      "id": 4,
      "gridPos": { "x": 0, "y": 26, "w": 24, "h": 6 },
      "options": {
        "content": "### How to Compare Single vs Multiple UE Registration Latency\n\n**Step 1: Baseline (Single UE)**\n1. Start with only 1 UE registration\n2. Observe \"Request Rate\" panel - should show low, steady rate (e.g., < 5 req/s per NF)\n3. Note the p95/p99 latency values in \"SBI Latency p95/p99\" panel\n4. Check \"Recent Traces\" - should see individual traces with low duration\n\n**Step 2: Load Test (Multiple UEs)**\n1. Trigger multiple UE registrations simultaneously (e.g., restart 5-10 UEs)\n2. Watch \"Request Rate\" spike (e.g., > 20 req/s)\n3. Observe if p95/p99 latency increases in correlation\n\n**Step 3: Analyze Impact**\n- **Key Panel**: \"Latency vs Request Rate Overlay\" - shows both metrics together\n  - If latency curve follows rate curve → system is load-sensitive\n  - If latency stays flat → good scalability\n- Check \"Top Slowest Traces\" to find which specific registration flows were affected\n- Use \"Per-NF p95 Comparison\" to identify which NF becomes bottleneck under load\n\n**Expected Behavior**:\n- **Good**: Latency increases slightly (< 2x) during load spikes\n- **Concerning**: Latency increases significantly (> 5x) or stays high after load drops\n\n**TraceQL Query Examples**:\n- All traces: `{}`\n- Slow traces: `{ duration > 100ms }`\n- Specific NF: `{resource.service.name=\"amf\"}`",
        "mode": "markdown"
      },
      "transparent": false
    }
  ],
